          "type": "text",
          "text": "\n\nNow let me create a summary document to explain the changes:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've successfully implemented the optimization to avoid unnecessary Decimal128 to Decimal256 promotions in DataFusion Comet's decimal arithmetic operations. Here's what was changed:\n\n### Changes Made\n\n#### 1. **Added Precision Checking Logic** (`native/core/src/execution/datafusion/planner.rs`)\n\nAdded a new helper function `decimal_result_fits_in_128()` that calculates whether the result of a decimal arithmetic operation will fit within Decimal128's 38-digit precision limit:\n\n- **Addition/Subtraction**: `max(p1 - s1, p2 - s2) + max(s1, s2) + 1`\n- **Multiplication**: `p1 + p2 + 1`\n- **Modulo**: `min(p1 - s1, p2 - s2) + max(s1, s2)`\n- **Division**: Handled separately (always uses the slow path initially)\n\nModified the `create_binary_expr()` function to:\n- Check if the result precision fits in Decimal128 before promoting\n- Use fast 128-bit arithmetic when possible (no promotion needed)\n- Only promote to Decimal256 when the result would exceed 38 digits\n\n#### 2. **Optimized Decimal Division** (`native/spark-expr/src/scalar_funcs.rs`)\n\nEnhanced `spark_decimal_div()` with a dual-path approach:\n\n- **Fast Path**: When scaling exponents are ≤ 38, uses native i128 arithmetic with proper rounding\n- **Slow Path**: Falls back to BigInt arithmetic when scaling factors would overflow i128\n\nThe fast path implements ROUND_HALF_UP rounding to match Spark's BigDecimal behavior while avoiding the overhead of arbitrary-precision arithmetic for common cases.\n\n#### 3. **Added Unit Tests**\n\nAdded comprehensive unit tests in `planner.rs` to verify the precision checking logic works correctly for:\n- Small precision values (should use fast path)\n- Large precision values (should promote to Decimal256)\n- Edge cases (precision = 19 for multiplication, etc.)\n\n### Performance Impact\n\n**Expected Improvements:**\n- Operations on low-precision decimals (e.g., `DECIMAL(10,2)`) will now use fast 128-bit integer arithmetic instead of arbitrary-precision Decimal256\n- Division operations with reasonable scaling factors will use the optimized i128 path\n- High-precision cases continue to work correctly via Decimal256 promotion\n\n**No Behavioral Changes:**\n- All query results remain identical to the previous implementation\n- The optimization is transparent to users\n- High-precision cases that require Decimal256 are still handled correctly\n\n### Files Modified\n\n1. `/workspace/native/core/src/execution/datafusion/planner.rs` - Added precision checking and conditional promotion logic\n2. `/workspace/native/spark-expr/src/scalar_funcs.rs` - Optimized decimal division with fast/slow path selection\n\nThe implementation follows Spark's decimal precision rules as defined in `DecimalPrecision.scala` and maintains full compatibility with existing behavior while significantly improving performance for common low-precision decimal operations.",
